1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,000
Open a photograph. Nothing leaves the machine.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,500
A preset is somebody's week of work.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,000
It sells on Monday and it is in a shared folder by the weekend. Nothing in the
file separates the copy somebody paid for from the one they did not.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Twenty-one tools, running on your own graphics card. Untouched, then graded.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,500
The frame is part of the recipe. Crop, straighten and keystone run in the first
pass, so grain and vignette land on the picture you keep.

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:33,000
Dodged, burned, and held to tone. Brush marks are stored as coordinates in the
photograph, not as a bitmap, so the recipe still fits in a link.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:41,000
The photograph stays put. Only the recipe moves — a few hundred bytes, hashed
from its own contents.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The upstream author is paid in the same transaction. Out of a 0.200 SOL sale,
0.010 goes upstream and 0.190 to the seller.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Leaving the first author off the transaction does not pay them less. It fails.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Six settlement checks against the live devnet program. Eighteen rendered
assertions on the editing pipeline. Three shader programs compiled before
anything ships.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,500
The render test found that every photograph had been coming out upside down.
It compiled, it typechecked, it built, and it was wrong.

12
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,000
grainroom-seven.vercel.app — running on Solana devnet.
